course: dictionary
lesson: 391
title: Saying sorry with words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To apologise means to say you are sorry. You can apologise for something you did wrong, or for a small trouble, like being late. «Connor is sent home and apologises next day, red-faced, his mum behind him.» Apologises here means he says sorry, and his red face shows he feels bad about what he did.
explain: Look at the words around apologise to find its meaning. «Maddie apologises to Ellie for laughing when she fell in the mud.» The word for tells you what the sorry is about: laughing at Ellie. Ellie's reply, «I'm never forgiving you, ever», shows that an apology does not always fix hurt feelings straight away.
explain: Apologise is a verb, so it changes its ending: I apologise, he apologises, they apologised, we are apologising. You can say apologise to someone, apologise for something, or just say I apologise to be polite.
fact: Apologise means to say you are sorry.
fact: You can apologise for something you did wrong, or for a small trouble like being late.
fact: «I apologise» is a polite way to say sorry.
fact: «Toad apologises fully» means he says sorry completely, not just a little.
fact: Saying sorry is part of good manners, and it can help to mend a friendship after a quarrel.
exercise: «Maddie apologises to Ellie for laughing when she fell in the mud.» What does apologises mean here? | Laughs again | *Says sorry | Runs away | Tells a joke
exercise: Which phrase is a polite way to say sorry for being late? | Hello, I am here | *I apologise for being late | Go away now | Whatever, I don't care
exercise: «Connor is sent home and apologises next day, red-faced, his mum behind him.» Find and copy the word that tells us Connor said sorry. | elbow | pushes | trampoline | *apologises
test: Ellie says, «I'm never forgiving you, ever.» What does forgiving mean? | Laughing about it | Saying sorry for something | *Stopping being angry about something | Getting muddy
test: Yesterday Sam was late, so today he ___ to his teacher. | sang | *apologised | laughed | slept
exam: Which sentence uses apologise correctly? | Ravi apologise his sister, sunny day. | *Ravi apologised to his sister for taking her book. | Ravi apologise running away from the bus. | Ravi apologised the bus for lunch.
